  abstract = {Electroencephalography (EEG) microstates are powerful tools for capturing transient brain states, yet their sequential organization remains insufficiently understood, limiting their application in affective disorder recognition. To address this issue, we propose a maximum probability model (MPM) to characterize the multi-scale temporal organization of EEG microstate sequences. Unlike conventional methods based on fixed-length segments or Markov assumptions, MPM adaptively extracts variable-length structural units from the statistical properties of microstate sequences in an unsupervised manner, thereby capturing richer temporal dependencies and latent sequential patterns. The results showed that microstate sequences tended to favor more predictable and structurally simpler patterns, as well as transitions toward longer segments. Further analyses indicated that microstate segments represented by structural features were significantly associated with clinical severity. To further investigate the potential application of MPM to affective disorder classification, we developed an MPM-based classification framework. The framework uses support vector machine decision margins to quantify discriminative confidence in the feature space, thereby integrating MPM-derived structural features with conventional static microstate features. Evaluation across the publicly available MODMA and EDRA datasets and the DBAH dataset demonstrated competitive classification performance. Overall, MPM imp},
}

