  abstract = {Snore sounds are clinically informative acoustic biomarkers, yet robust and transparent automatic classification remains challenging.We propose an explainable Siamese framework for four-class snore classification that treats similarity as decision evidence.A lightweight convolution module learns ℓ2normalised embeddings using joint semi-hard triplet and classbalanced cross-entropy training.Classification is performed via a non-parametric Class Centroid aligned with metric learning.A support-based explanation mechanism relates predictions to reference examples and visually comparable time-frequency activation patterns.On the Munich-Passau Snore Sound Corpus, macro-recall improves from 0.401 (wav2vec2) to 0.638 and remains robust under additive noise.Deletion and perturbation tests validate explanation faithfulness and stability, demonstrating interpretable similarity-based reasoning for clinical sleep assessment.},
