  abstract = {By Bin Hu, Guest Editor Pervasive Computing envisions a world of communicating small and potentially portable devices, sensors, and actuators. Actually, it is a rapidly developing area of Information and Communications Technology. The term refers to the increasing integration of Information and Communications Technology (ICT) into people's life and environments, made possible by the growing availability of microprocessors with inbuilt communication facilities. Pervasive computing has many potential applications, from health and home care to environment monitoring and intelligent transport systems. This briefing provides an overview of pervasive computing technologies and discusses the growing debate over privacy, safety, and environmental implications. Among the myriad of tools expected to support there are application-specific integrated circuitry; speech recognition; gesture recognition; system on a chip; perceptive interfaces; smart matter; flexible transistors; reconfigurable processors; field programmable logic gates; and microelectromechanical systems. This special issue showcases papers that describe recent research in these domains. Let the contents of the articles be briefly summarized. In “A novel task scheduling algorithm based on dynamic critical path and effective duplication for pervasive computing environment,” Junzhou Luo, Fang Dong, Jiuxi Cao, and Aibo Song propose a novel task scheduling algorithm based on dynamic critical path (DCP) and effective duplicatio},
